mido: say which userspace fault killed the process

Both remaining boot shapes end in a userspace death, and neither one leaves
enough on the console to act on.  The common shape is:

  MIDO SR 0126: noslide, slide 0x4c830000 removed from 0 mapping(s) in 0
      file(s); first mapping 0x1cc830000 -> 0x180000000 (base 0x180000000)
  MIDO SR: launchd check_np -> 0x180000000
  pid 1 exited -- exit reason namespace 2 subcode 0xb, description none
  panic: initproc failed to start -- exit reason namespace 2 subcode 0xb

namespace 2 is OS_REASON_SIGNAL and subcode 0xb is SIGSEGV, so the shared
cache now sits at its nominal base and dyld's own arithmetic agrees with it,
but launchd still faults on something.  Nothing in that message says what:
no crash reporter runs, ReportCrash cannot even exec (its signature is
rejected), and the kernel never prints the faulting address.

Handle it where the answer still exists -- handle_user_abort(), right after
vm_fault() has failed and before the EXC_BAD_ACCESS is turned into a signal:

  MIDO UFAULT 1: pid 1 kr 0x5 addr 0x1801a2340 prot 0x1 pc 0x1800b0f20 ...
                        ^ KERN_PROTECTION_FAILURE   ^ faulting address   ^ pc

pc inside the shared cache range (0x180000000-0x1ffffffff) means dyld/launchd
read a cache page that is mapped but not what it expected; pc inside the
process's own __TEXT means it is launchd's or a library's own code; lr says
who called it.  Ten lines like that identify the failure far better than the
signal number alone.

Bounded to 64 reports: when launchd dies it takes a dozen daemons with it and
the console ring only holds about 2000 lines.
--- a/osfmk/arm64/sleh.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/sleh.c
@@ -42,6 +42,7 @@
 #include <kern/debug.h>
 #include <kern/restartable.h>
 #include <kern/socd_client.h>
+#include <kern/task.h>
 #include <kern/thread.h>
 #include <kern/zalloc_internal.h>
 #include <mach/exception.h>
@@ -1410,6 +1411,33 @@
 		}
 
 		/*
+		 * mido: the VM could not satisfy this fault, so the thread is about
+		 * to take an EXC_BAD_ACCESS and, with nothing to catch it, die of
+		 * SIGSEGV/SIGBUS.  There is no crash reporter on this port, and the
+		 * process's own death message carries nothing but a signal number:
+		 *
+		 *   pid 1 exited -- exit reason namespace 2 subcode 0xb
+		 *
+		 * Print the context while it is still in registers.  Bounded, because
+		 * a dead launchd takes a dozen daemons with it.
+		 */
+		{
+			static unsigned int mido_ufault_reports;
+
+			if (mido_ufault_reports < 64) {
+				mido_ufault_reports++;
+				kprintf("MIDO UFAULT %u: pid %d kr 0x%x addr 0x%llx "
+				    "prot 0x%x pc 0x%llx lr 0x%llx sp 0x%llx esr 0x%x\n",
+				    mido_ufault_reports, task_pid(thread->task),
+				    result, (unsigned long long)vm_fault_addr,
+				    fault_type,
+				    (unsigned long long)get_saved_state_pc(state),
+				    (unsigned long long)get_saved_state_lr(state),
+				    (unsigned long long)get_saved_state_sp(state), esr);
+			}
+		}
+
+		/*
 		 * vm_fault() should never return KERN_FAILURE for page faults from user space.
 		 * If it does, we're leaking preemption disables somewhere in the kernel.
 		 */
